.TH STASH 1 "2026-09-02" "stash" "STASH User Commands"
.SH NAME
stash \- named filesets on cache storage, warmed from a source storage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stash
[\fIGLOBAL OPTIONS\fR]
.I COMMAND
[\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
STASH stages data for HPC jobs. A
.B fileset
is a named directory on a cache storage with a reserved size. A
.B cached
fileset is filled from a source storage and can be rebuilt from it; an
.B output
fileset is created empty, written by jobs, and exists nowhere else until it is flushed.
.PP
Every decision \(em whether an allocation fits, what a transfer will cost, where a
fileset lives \(em belongs to the server. This client asks and reports.
.SH REFERENCES
.TP
.B STORAGE:/path
A path inside a storage system. The leading slash is what makes it a path.
.TP
.B STORAGE:name
A fileset on a storage system.
.TP
.B name
A fileset on the default storage, from \fB--storage\fR, \fBSTASH_STORAGE\fR or the
config file. With no default, the command lists the storages it could have meant and
exits 2; it never guesses.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-server \fIURL\fR
The controller. Also \fBSTASH_SERVER\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-storage \fIID\fR
Default storage for bare fileset names.
.TP
.B \-\-json
One JSON object on stdout and nothing else.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Never colour the output. \fBNO_COLOR\fR does the same.
.TP
.B \-\-timeout \fISECONDS\fR
Per-request timeout.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Do not ask before anything destructive.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Say less. \fB-v\fR says more.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Reading
.TP
.B stash whoami
The user, their groups, whether they are an admin, and the server version.
.TP
.B stash locations
The sites the cluster spans.
.TP
.B stash storages
Storages with their roles, tiers, drivers, drain state and daemon health.
.TP
.B stash fileset list \fR[\fB\-\-storage \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-user \fIU\fR] [\fB\-\-kind \fIcached|output\fR] [\fB\-\-state \fIS\fR] [\fB\-l\fR]
Filesets with what they reserve and what they use.
.B stash list
is the same command.
.TP
.B stash fileset show \fIFILESET\fR
One fileset: where it is, what it holds, and every transfer that touched it.
.TP
.B stash quota \fR[\fB\-\-user \fIU\fR] [\fB\-\-storage \fIS\fR]
Limits, allocation and usage per cache, and cluster-wide.
.TP
.B stash path \fIFILESET\fR
The absolute path, one line, nothing else. Exit 4 if the fileset does not exist or is
not READY, so a job script can branch on it.
.SS Moving data
.TP
.B stash warm \fISOURCE TARGET\fR [\fB\-\-size \fISIZE\fR] [\fB\-\-refresh\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-wait \fR[\fB\-\-timeout \fIT\fR]]
Fill a cached fileset from a path on a source storage, creating it if the name is free.
\fB--dry-run\fR shows the preflight and submits nothing.
.TP
.B stash fileset create \fITARGET\fB \-\-size \fISIZE\fR
An empty output fileset with a reserved size.
.TP
.B stash fileset resize \fIFILESET\fB \-\-size \fISIZE\fR
Change what a fileset reserves. Shrinking asks first.
.TP
.B stash cool \fIFILESET\fR [\fB\-\-to \fISTORAGE:/path\fR] [\fB\-\-keep\fR] [\fB\-\-discard\fR] [\fB\-\-wait\fR]
Write a fileset out, release it, or both. With \fB--to\fR the fileset is flushed and then
released unless \fB--keep\fR. Without \fB--to\fR it is released; an output fileset holds
the only copy of its data, so the server refuses unless \fB--discard\fR says the data is
not wanted.
.TP
.B stash release \fIFILESET\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]
The destructive half of \fBcool\fR, said plainly. \fB--force\fR is \fB--discard\fR.
.SS Transfers
.TP
.B stash status \fR[\fITRANSFER_ID...\fR] [\fB\-\-user \fIU\fR] [\fB\-\-state \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-watch \fR[\fB\-\-interval \fIN\fR]]
Your transfers, or the ones named. \fB--watch\fR polls to a terminal state: a bar on a
terminal, a line per poll in a job log. Ctrl-C detaches without cancelling.
.TP
.B stash queue \fR[\fB\-\-storage \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-route \fIR\fR] [\fB\-\-user \fIU\fR] [\fB\-\-limit \fIN\fR]
What everyone is waiting for.
.TP
.B stash cancel \fITRANSFER_ID...\fR
Stop transfers. What has already arrived stays where it is.
.SS Administration
These need admin rights; the server decides, not the client.
.TP
.B stash admin limit set \fIUSER\fR [\fISTORAGE\fR] \fISIZE\fR
Set a per-storage or cluster-wide allocation limit. Lowering one below what a user
already holds blocks their next allocation and deletes nothing.
.TP
.B stash admin limit unset \fIUSER\fR [\fISTORAGE\fR]
Clear a limit, so the configured default applies again.
.TP
.B stash admin report usage \fR[\fB\-\-group\-by \fIuser|storage|route|location\fR] [\fB\-\-since \fID\fR] [\fB\-\-until \fID\fR]
What was transferred in a window.
.TP
.B stash admin report allocation
Who holds what, and whose filesets are past what they reserved.
.TP
.B stash admin drain \fISTORAGE\fR
Stop new work on a storage; what is running finishes.
.B stash admin undrain \fISTORAGE\fR
lets it take work again.
.SH SIZES
Input takes IEC and SI suffixes and they differ:
.B 500Gi
is 500 \(mu 1024\u3\d,
.B 500G
is 500 \(mu 1000\u3\d. Human output is IEC with one decimal;
.B \-\-json
always carries raw bytes.
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Generic or unexpected failure. Also a \fB--wait\fR that ran out of \fB--timeout\fR: the
transfer is still running.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Authentication failed; check that munged is running.
.TP
.B 4
Not found, or not ready.
.TP
.B 5
Allocation or capacity exceeded.
.TP
.B 6
Conflict, or not permitted.
.TP
.B 7
Server or storage unavailable.
.TP
.B 8
A transfer failed, with \fB--wait\fR.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B STASH_SERVER
Controller URL.
.TP
.B STASH_STORAGE
Default storage for bare fileset names.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Any value turns colour off.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/stash/config.toml
Per-user configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/stash/stashcli.toml
System-wide configuration.
.SH COMPLETION
.B stash \-\-install\-completion
installs completion for the current shell;
.B stash \-\-show\-completion
prints it. Storage ids and fileset names are completed from the server.
.SH SEE ALSO
The STASH operations guide, for what an operator has to decide and watch.
